agent-skills — provenance and licensing
=======================================

This repository bundles Agent Skills from several upstream sources under different
licenses. Each skill directory's own LICENSE.txt or NOTICE governs that skill. First-party
skills authored here are released under the MIT License.

Known upstreams
---------------

- Zenobi dotfiles release-please skill (zenobi-us/dotfiles, MIT) — adopted at
  027c8c80863883533ad1a0fc05669c482fc9a89d. Bundled under release-please/,
  adapted for 0.x policy, release approval gates and current action version.
  See release-please/NOTICE and release-please/LICENSE.txt.

- Anthropic Skills (anthropics/skills, MIT) — e.g. docx, pdf, pptx, xlsx, canvas-design,
  frontend-design, mcp-builder, brand-guidelines, artifacts tooling, skill-creator,
  slack-gif-creator, webapp-testing. Each carries its upstream LICENSE.txt.

- Superpowers (obra/superpowers) — brainstorming, dispatching-parallel-agents,
  executing-plans, finishing-a-development-branch, receiving-code-review,
  requesting-code-review, subagent-driven-development, systematic-debugging,
  test-driven-development, using-git-worktrees, writing-plans, writing-skills. The three
  named core-adjacent skills were rewritten in this repository; the remaining listed skills
  retain more of the upstream workflow and are not in the default core profile.

- OpenAI Agents SDK repo skills (openai/openai-agents-python, .agents/skills, MIT):
    * large-change-review/            adapts the tier model and diff-walkthrough discipline
                                      of `implementation-final-review` (and the
                                      task-boundary / merge-base opening of
                                      `implementation-kickoff`). Provenance and the exact
                                      commit are recorded in large-change-review/NOTICE.
    * automated-testing-workflow/scripts/verify.sh, verify.ps1
                                      adapt the sequential fail-fast gate pattern of
                                      `code-change-verification`. Provenance is in each
                                      script header.

